iven that  sort of big picture motivation  i'll be talking about three things today  first i'll motivate the use of this new  method deep neural networks to study  auditory coding  um then i'll show that these dnns uh in  fact perform better than standard widely  used encoding models for describing  activity in auditory cortex in  particular the time-varying activity  that's that occurs during presentation  of natural sounds  then finally i'll get it a challenging  question that that we encounter with  dnns which is uh how to interpret them  and so i'll spend some time showing that  that we've some methods we've developed  can be used to understand what it is  that these new models are doing  so as i mentioned there's been  substantial effort previously to build  encoding models in the auditory system  as well as other sensory systems and  in the auditory system the majority of  models are based on the idea of  spectrotemporal representations  so it's well established that in the  cochlea different populations of  receptors  respond to sounds at different  frequencies so  you can model this peripheral activity  as what's called the sound spectrogram  which is shown at the bottom here where  each row shows the sort of level of  activation in response to a sound at a  different frequency  and as you can see with this example  segment of speech natural sounds contain  complex spectral patterns and the job of  the auditory system is to recognize and  discriminate these patterns  that have different behavioral meanings  and so what you might call the standard  model for sound coding in in auditory  neurons is the linear non-linear  spectrotemporal receptive field often  called the strf  according to this model each neuron is  sensitive to stimuli in a particular  range of frequencies  and time lags this is illustrated here  by the heat map in the upper right  where red end areas indicate features  that increase spiking activity and blue  areas  when they're present indicate regions or  features that suppress spiking activity  using the strf the activity of a neuron  can be predicted by convolving this  linear filter with the sound spectrogram  the the output of the filter is followed  by what's called a static nonlinearity  which accounts for the fact that neurons  cannot produce negative action  potentials  and so this is used in the auditory  system but it's also used more widely  this general formulation is used more  widely across sensory systems and it's  often referred to as the linear  nonlinear ln model  and in the case of this neuron you can  see that the ln model is doing a pretty  good job so the the the panel at the  bottom shows the prediction of the ln  model in red  and  you can see that it corresponds pretty  well to the actual response that was  evoked in the neuron that was being  studied here which is shown in blue  so the ion sgrf is appealing uh because  it's relatively easy to understand  what's going on you can you can  look at the linear filter and you can  see what sound features are encoded by  the neuron and so this can be used for  instance to measure the best frequency  or the bandwidth of the  responses of the neurons and  using this approach uh a number of  studies have developed basic models of  what's going on in the auditory  periphery and how sounds are represented  at that stage of processing  however the problem uh with the ln model  becomes a little bit  bigger in more central areas especially  in auditory cortex because what happens  is these models don't predict everything  that the sounds actually produce  and so you can see that in this example  where again we have an strf  the color map is changed here but the  red and blue areas still indicate  excitatory and inhibitory tuning  and in this example we use the ln model  to predict the response to uh from a  different neuron to predict response to  a natural vocalization  and  in the in this uh  this data set the actual response is  plotted 