uditory system as well  and and the question here today is can  we use these approaches to describe the  activity of single units in in auditory  cortex  and so just to kind of point out again  and emphasize uh the kind of differences  between these different approaches to  modeling  now you can think of of the the ln model  as kind of being at one end or near one  end of the encoding model spectrum where  they're relatively simple and so while  they don't generalize uh to a large  number of stimuli very effectively they  are relatively easy to interpret  and these more complex models that have  been developed  are generally able to predict activity  in a wider range of circumstances but  they are also more complex to fit and  they require more data for fitting  and and these deep networks or sort of  the general class of artificial neural  networks lies a kind of  the extreme case where they have the  most generalizability but also are the  most complex  and with this complexity becomes a  challenge of interpretation and this is  something that is  particularly important for understanding  really the computations that are taking  place because these models are very  large and complex it's difficult to tell  exactly what information uh they're  they're representing in the neural  computations  so as i mentioned the dnns have been  applied uh most successfully and  extensively in the visual system  and this diagram here taken from the  work of yemen's and dicarlo uh  illustrates why it's very appealing as a  model for  sensory processing particularly in  sensory systems where there is an idea  that information is processed by a  hierarchical sequence of sensory areas  and so each dot here in these squares at  the top indicate a a a  cluster of uh simulated neurons each one  really is  effectively an uh  implementation of an ln model and so you  have these layers of of ln neurons each  with slightly different tuning that feed  into subsequent layers and and  what has 