s what we call a  single unit dnn we also developed what  we call a population model where we had  a similar architecture but instead of  just predicting the activity of a single  unit we had a large output layer that  that combined the previous layer with  different weights in order to predict  the activity of each of the neurons in  the population  and what we found is that this could  leverage the activity of a large number  of neurons into a common representation  that kind of took advantage of this  additional statistical power to get a  better prediction accuracy  and we used two different architectures  for the input layers  the what we call the one deconvolution  was mimicking in the classic strf model  the filters that i discussed already at  the input and then we also used a 2d  convolution which is more analogous to  the models that have been used in the  visual system and auditory system  previously  so  we evaluated a large number of models  that varied in the size and number of  layers i don't have time to go into all  the details today but actually as we  found the performance of these models  was very similar independent of the  architecture so those details may  actually not be so important  for fitting we used the neural encoding  model system library which was developed  as part of the brain initiative  that supports this project  and  the multiple fit using backpropagation  implemented in a tensorflow backend  so how does it do  well here's an example of the neuron  that i showed you previously uh where  the actual response shows uh the sort of  complex temporal dynamics uh when we  predict responses with this lm model it  fails to predict those responses so then  when we used a dnn model  again  implemented here this was using a 1d  convolution in the architectures that i  mentioned  it was able to capture quite a bit of  the detail in the in the temporal  response  as you can see in this purple plot  that's overlapped at the bottom  and across 