ate regions or  features that suppress spiking activity  using the strf the activity of a neuron  can be predicted by convolving this  linear filter with the sound spectrogram  the the output of the filter is followed  by what's called a static nonlinearity  which accounts for the fact that neurons  cannot produce negative action  potentials  and so this is used in the auditory  system but it's also used more widely  this general formulation is used more  widely across sensory systems and it's  often referred to as the linear  nonlinear ln model  and in the case of this neuron you can  see that the ln model is doing a pretty  good job so the the the panel at the  bottom shows the prediction of the ln  model in red  and  you can see that it corresponds pretty  well to the actual response that was  evoked in the neuron that was being  studied here which is shown in blue  so the ion sgrf is appealing uh because  it's relatively easy to understand  what's going on you can you can  look at the linear filter and you can  see what sound features are encoded by  the neuron and so this can be used for  instance to measure the best frequency  or the bandwidth of the  responses of the neurons and  using this approach uh a number of  studies have developed basic models of  what's going on in the auditory  periphery and how sounds are represented  at that stage of processing  however the problem uh with the ln model  becomes a little bit  bigger in more central areas especially  in auditory cortex because what happens  is these models don't predict everything  that the sounds actually produce  and so you can see that in this example  where again we have an strf  the color map is changed here but the  red and blue areas still indicate  excitatory and inhibitory tuning  and in this example we use the ln model  to predict the response to uh from a  different neuron to predict response to  a natural vocalization  and  in the in this uh  this data set the actual response is  plotted in gray and you can see that  there are periods of brief activation  associated with the onset and offset of  different sound features  and the model the ln model prediction is  overlaid in orange you can see in this  case it predicts the course activation  in response to different sound features  but it fails to predict this fine  temporal structure  and across auditory cortex the the  performance the accuracy of prediction  by the ln models is limited so we can  quantify prediction accuracy with the  correlation coefficient or r value  between the predicted and actual time  varying response  using this metric a maximum value of 1  indicates a perfect prediction  and  the r squared value indicates the  fraction of variance explained in the  neural response  and so in in one study after accounting  for a number of experimental factors  that can limit model performance uh we  found that the sort of maximal  performance on average across neurons  and auditory cortex  explains about 40 of response variants  and so there is room for improvement  over these models  and so this this problem is well known  and a number of efforts have been made  in past decades to develop models that  can perform better than the sgrf  and so i don't have time to go into all  the details of these models because  there are quite a few and they've taken  different approaches to extending the  strff either using ideas of different  biophysical properties present in the  system or using more general approaches  to expand beyond the linear  representations to various nonlinear  interactions that can be observed  between features  and so one  class of model that has grown uh  very popular recently in a number of  different applications is a new machine  learning approach uh called deep  learning or using deep neural networks  to model these responses and so these uh  these models have been developed and and  applied largely in the visual system so  far and with a little bit of work done  in the auditory system as well  and and the question here today is can  we use these approaches to describe the  activity of single units in in auditory  cortex  and so just to kind of point out again  and emphasize uh the kind of differences  between these different approaches to  modeling  now you can think of of the the ln model  as kind of being at one end or near one  end of the encoding model spectrum where  they're relatively simple and so while  they don't generalize uh to a large  number of stimuli very effectively they  are relatively easy to interpret  and these more complex models that have  been developed  are generally able to predict activity  in a wider range of circumstances but  they are also more complex to fit and  they require more data for fitting  and and these deep networks or sort of  the general class of artificial neural  networks lies a kind of  the extreme case where they have the  most generalizability but also are the  most complex  and with this complexity becomes a  challenge of interpretation and this is  something that is  particularly important for understanding  really the computations that are taking  place because these models are very  large and complex it's difficult to tell  exactly what information uh they're  they're representing in the neural  computations  so as i mentioned the dnns have been  applied uh most successfully and  extensively in the visual system  and this diagram here taken from the  work of yemen's and dicarlo uh  illustrates why it's very appealing as a  model for  sensory processing particularly in  sensory systems where there is an idea  that information is processed by a  hierarchical sequence of sensory areas  and so each dot here in these squares at  the top indicate a a a  cluster of uh simulated neurons each one  really is  effectively an uh  implementation of an ln model and so you  have these layers of of ln neurons each  with slightly different tuning that feed  into subsequent layers and and  what has 