his idea of  laminar hierarchy where you can back  project activity from either earlier  layers which are shown at left which  produce uh features that are related to  features that have been proposed to  occur at mid-level representations in  the visual system  and if you back protect the late stages  what you get are features that actually  look like visual objects uh that are uh  believed to be associated with activity  in in these central processing areas  dn analysis has not been applied as much  in the auditory system  but recently our collaborators used  the same general approach to study  encoding in human auditory cortex using  lfp data recorded from human epilepsy  patients undergoing monitoring for  treatment  and they used a a  similar architecture  of these layers of ln units as they're  illustrated in the bottom of this  picture here and what they found is that  when they compared performance to a  traditional ln model as is shown in the  scatter plot at the left that the  dnn performed consistently better both  in primary auditory cortex which is  heschel's gyrus or hg as well as in  superior temporal gyrus which is a  tertiary or higher higher order  representation  in human auditory cortex  and so the question we had today was can  we use the same modeling approach to  understand the activity of single units  in in the auditory cortex of  animals in a similar experimental  condition  so  the next question here is well can we  describe the time time-varying activity  of neurons in auditory cortex  briefly to describe our experimental  approach  we were presenting a large library of  natural sounds about 600 different  one-second samples as well as a  set of repeated sounds used for model  validation uh we presented these to  awake passively listening ferrets uh  using free field stimulation  contralateral to the recording site  and to collect single unit activity we  used laminar probes  6428 128  channel probes from ucla uh that uh  permitted uh simultaneous recording of  somewhere between 20 and 50 single units  in an experiment uh we could identify uh  lamina in different cortical fields  although we're not talking about the the  specific uh laminar patterns today  so in the ferret there's a hierarchy of  auditory areas that's roughly matched to  those that are primates  auditory stimuli come into the midbrain  they're processed  at several stages including the inferior  colliculus then pass through primary  auditory cortex or a1  and then some non-primary areas called  the belts uh and then ferrets it's  called area peg eventually that  information gets  switched or believed to guide behavioral  decisions  the data i'm talking about today was  recorded from two areas a1 which is  primary auditory cortex as well as this  belt region term peg  so we presented the same stimuli in each  experiment uh the same large library of  natural sounds and so as i mentioned we  get record from multiple neurons in one  experiment but then what we could also  do is construct a pseudo population by  combining the data from all of these  experiments into one large data set  that merged all of these different  recording sites and again we had  multiple sites from both a1 and the  secondary area bg  and the details of the models that we  tested we used a standard linear  nonlinear model as a baseline  and then we explored a number of  different dnn architectures  to kind of build on the previous results  we used a small dnn to analyze the  activity of of individual single units  that were recorded in the study and this  diagram here just shows roughly the  layout of the layers and arrangement of  the linear and non-linear units  the linear units were linear filters at  the input or dense uh dense units at  later layers  and the non-linearity in this case is  just a rectified uh  a linear rectifier uh which we chose for  reasons that may become apparent uh when  we talk about visualization in a few  slides  so in addition to this what we call a  single unit dnn we also developed what  we call a population model where we had  a similar architecture but instead of  just predicting the activity of a single  unit we had a large output layer that  that combined the previous layer with  different weights in order to predict  the activity of each of the neurons in  the population  and what we found is that this could  leverage the activity of a large number  of neurons into a common representation  that kind of took advantage of this  additional statistical power to get a  better prediction accuracy  and we used two different architectures  for the input layers  the what we call the one deconvolution  was mimicking in the classic strf model  the filters that i discussed already at  the input and then we also used a 2d  convolution which is more analogous to  the models that have been used in the  visual system and auditory system  previously  so  we evaluated a large number of models  that varied in the size and number of  layers i don't have time to go into all  the details today but actually as we  found the performance of these models  was very similar independent of the  architecture so those details may  actually not be so important  for fitting we used the neural encoding  model system library which was developed  as part of the brain initiative  that supports this project  and  the multiple fit using backpropagation  implemented in a tensorflow backend  so how does it do  well here's an example of the neuron  that i showed you previously uh where  the actual response shows uh the sort of  complex temporal dynamics uh when we  predict responses with this lm model it  fails to predict those responses so then  when we used a dnn model  again  implemented here this was using a 1d  convolution in the architectures that i  mentioned  it was able to capture quite a bit of  the detail in the in the temporal  response  as you can see in this purple plot  that's overlapped at the bottom  and across 