eurons where one  model would perform better than the  other but overall they're actually  predicting with very very similar  accuracy  and we can also  compare the  what we call the equivalence of these  different models by simply computing the  correlation coefficient between the  predicted responses of the different  models and so if we do that for the ln  model versus  one of the dna models that's plotted in  the orange histogram  you can see that the correlations are  generally  relatively low lower than one  but in the case of the two different dnn  architectures which are plotted in blue  you can see that they're significantly  higher and often very close to one which  indicates that they're that the  predictions are actually identical  so  as i mentioned we compared performance  of a large number of different  formulations of of the dnn architecture  and this pareto plot  shows the average performance the median  performance across all the neurons that  we recorded from an a1  and the x-axis shows the what we call  the complexity of the model which is  basically the number of free parameters  that were required per neuron and the  model fits  and what you might note uh  most prominently is that the model at  the bottom is the the performance of the  ln model which peaked at a course  five  and that didn't matter if we analyzed  each neuron separately or analyzed them  as a population model similar to the  population models that we're using for  the dnn  on the other hand all all the dnn models  performed quite similarly and  this includes again both the 1d  convolution as well as the 2d  convolution architecture and a number of  different variants that are that are  sort of schematized here  one thing to note is that the single  neuron dnn  achieved almost as good performance but  required generally a larger number of  parameters per neuron in order to  achieve the same level of performance  we performed the same analysis on the  data that we collected from 