or or the  brightness of the line indicates the  strength of that weight  so  you can see that there's a lot going on  and we can actually look at the  prediction of the model on the right  we're comparing the population  prediction uh to the actual response  that we saw in the bottom panel you can  see that there's very roughly a  correspondence of these the predicted  natural responses  but really understanding what's going on  here is tricky because there's just so  many there's so many free parameters and  so much going on  so what we did is use an approach that  was developed by our collaborators and  applied previously to the human lfp data  and that took advantage of a  relatively simple but important  observation of these networks which is  that at any given moment in time when  you're presenting stimuli a number of  the static non-linearities these  rectified linearities that i mentioned  earlier are below threshold so basically  the input is not  driving their outputs and so they're  effectively absent from the activity of  the of the of the network in this  particular state  and  so what we can do is we can essentially  remove those nodes and what's left is  the activated nodes which are all  performing just simple linear  transformations  and so the net activity of the of the  dnn at any given moment in time can  actually be collapsed into a single  linear filter which looks just like the  strf that we presented previously  uh but it's important to remember of  course that this is a dynamic strf so  the specific strf will change depending  on the particular stimulus that you're  presenting  and so just to get a very simple  intuition for this uh you can think of  the stimulus response relationship for  for a neural network or any nonlinear  system as this  this  in this cartoon example some some curve  that that's not flat right that it's  that's a nonlinear function  and if we look at some small regime of  stimulation for instance the yellow or  the blue areas here  the performance within that regime is  can be approximated using a line  and so what we're doing when we compute  a dstrf is effectively fitting this  locally linear model to the activity in  this sort of restricted sensory context  and so what does that look like with  with the data that we have so here's  that first example that i showed you and  there's there's quite a bit going on  here uh but what you might recognize at  the top is the stimulus and then the  prediction of the ln model and then the  dnn model in purple  and what i'm showing in the bottom row  are dynamic strfs that work fit  again using at each point in time that's  indicated by those dashed red lines  and so what you might notice is that uh  the pattern of activation the the area  the blue the red and blue areas that  indicate excitatory inhibitory tuning  are changing from moment to moment  and  so what this illustrates is that  while the ln strf shows a single pattern  of activation that actually this neuron  is responding to different  spectrogeneral patterns at different  points in time  but if you look carefully at this data  what you'll see is that it's not  completely random so the dsgrfs are  changing but they're actually changing  in somewhat repeatable ways and so you  can see that by performing a principal  component analysis or pca on the dsgrs  and then the first three pcs are shown  at the bottom here and what they show  is that there is a relatively  small set of features that explain the  variations that we're seeing in the  dsgrf across time  so  uh we use this dstrf analysis and then  perform pca on the cells in our in our  uh  data set and here are just ten examples  showing uh at the top the first pc the  largest pc and then the bottom two rows  show the second and third pcs for those  same neurons  and again there's a lot to look at here  and this is an area that we're actively  exploring to try to understand what  information is captured by these neurons  but